        "id": "ch4_4_2-1",
        "text": "Appearance\nNot only can you change the desktop background, but you can also change the\ncoloring used in application windows themselves. From the Settings tool, click the\nAppearance option in the left-hand menu. The Appearance window appears, as\nshown in Figure 4-11.\nIn the Window Colors section, the Appearance option lets you set the shading of\napplication windows: Choose light, standard, or dark.\nBeneath that are some settings you can change to control the dash (referred to as\ndock in the Settings). If your Linux distribution displays the dash on the desktop,\nyou can choose to auto-hide it when an application wants to go to full screen\nmode. This feature allows applications to push the dash out of the way and take up\nthe full desktop viewing area.\nFrom here you can also change the size of the application icons that appear in the\ndash (both the favorites and running applications), as well as set the side of the\ndesktop that the dash appears when you open the activities overview (left, right,\ntop, or bottom).\nCHAPTER 4 Examining the GNOME Desktop 77",
